@* M-D3 owner ruling (U2), progress.md near-verbatim: "U2 xref expansion --
   down: +2 refs; up: -2; double-down: all; double-up: collapse to
   original." Extended by U6 to Catechism ("Catechism defaults to 2 shown
   + U2 mechanics") -- ONE component, parameterized, backing BOTH lists
   ("one component/behavior, parameterized -- never two implementations,"
   the brief's own words, the identical discipline PASSAGE-1/
   CAP-RECONCILE-1 already establish elsewhere in this popover platform).
   PassageList.razor uses this INTERNALLY for its own cross-references/
   THE SCRIPTURES/place est-dest reveal (REPLACES its retired binary
   Cap/_showAll all-or-nothing toggle); CatechismSeamSection uses it
   DIRECTLY (its own list is a plain button row, not a PassageList/
   PassageSourceUnit consumer, so it cannot go through that component --
   this one is deliberately shaped generically enough for either caller:
   a total count, a floor, a step, and a currently-shown count, nothing
   about WHAT is being shown).

   INTERACTION: a SINGLE click on the down arrow reveals Step MORE (never
   past Total); a single click on the up arrow collapses Step FEWER
   (never below Default -- "never below the default," the brief's own
   words).

   "DOUBLE-DOWN"/"DOUBLE-UP" -- DISCLOSED DEVIATION FROM THE LITERAL
   WORDING, a real, live-caught, reproducible bug forced this: a genuine
   native DOUBLE-CLICK gesture was tried first (both sub-clicks of a
   dblclick target ONE fixed screen coordinate), but THIS popover platform
   re-centers/repositions its OWN outer panel as its content's height
   changes (every popover here is viewport- or pane-centered, R3's own
   verse-anchored mode included) -- so the FIRST click's own Reveal()
   growing the list by Step entries shifts the WHOLE panel, and with it
   this arrow's own on-screen position, before the second sub-click of the
   SAME gesture lands. Reordering these controls to lead the list (see
   PassageList.razor's/CatechismList.razor's own matching comment)
   stabilizes their position RELATIVE to the list, but not the panel's own
   ABSOLUTE screen position, which still moves with it. Observed live,
   repeatedly, not merely theorized: the second sub-click landing on a
   newly-revealed passage entry or catechism item instead of the arrow,
   PUSHING THAT NODE and navigating the whole popover away from what the
   user was actually trying to expand -- a materially worse outcome than
   the shortcut simply not firing. A SHIFT-CLICK realizes the identical
   "jump to the far end" semantic with exactly ONE physical click/dispatch
   -- structurally immune to this hazard, since there is no second,
   separately-targeted screen coordinate to go stale. Every arrow's own
   `title` attribute spells out the shortcut as a native hover tooltip,
   the same disclosure a dblclick shortcut would have needed anyway (it is
   otherwise as undiscoverable as this app's every other reveal control,
   e.g. place-card-more/-collapse, always was); the plain, single-step
   click remains the PRIMARY, fully keyboard-reachable path either way.
   MouseEventArgs.ShiftKey (native to Blazor's own @onclick binding, no JS
   interop needed) is read directly -- no second event handler, no
   competing dispatch.

   CONDITIONAL PRESENCE: renders nothing at all when Total &lt;= Default
   (nothing to reveal, same "no arrow at all under the cap" rule XREF-1
   always followed); the collapse arrow is independently conditional on
   Shown &gt; Default, the more arrow on Shown &lt; Total -- BOTH can render
   together in a genuine middle state (this component's own incremental
   model has one, unlike the retired all-or-nothing toggle, which never
   showed both at once). *@
@if (Total > Default)
{
    <div class="popover-reveal-controls">
        @if (Shown > Default)
        {
            <button type="button" class="popover-reveal-arrow popover-reveal-collapse" data-testid="@CollapseTestId"
                    aria-label="@($"Show fewer {RevealNoun}")"
                    title="@($"Show {Math.Min(Step, Shown - Default)} fewer {RevealNoun} -- Shift-click to collapse to the default view")"
                    @onclick="OnCollapseClick">&#9652;</button>
        }
        else
        {
            @* A same-sized, empty placeholder -- NOT the button, kept
               entirely out of the DOM when absent (`.toHaveCount(0)` stays
               a true "does not exist" check for CollapseTestId, the same
               conditional-presence guarantee every other reveal arrow in
               this app already gives). Its ONLY job is to hold the collapse
               arrow's own slot at a fixed width regardless of whether the
               real button is present -- see the more-arrow's own slot,
               .popover-reveal-slot, for why this matters. *@
            <span class="popover-reveal-slot"></span>
        }
        @if (Shown < Total)
        {
            <button type="button" class="popover-reveal-arrow popover-reveal-more" data-testid="@MoreTestId"
                    aria-label="@($"Show {Math.Min(Step, Total - Shown)} more {RevealNoun}")"
                    title="@($"Show {Math.Min(Step, Total - Shown)} more {RevealNoun} -- Shift-click to show all {Total}")"
                    @onclick="OnMoreClick">&#9662;</button>
        }
    </div>
}

@code {
    // The list's own true total size -- never re-derived from Shown/Step,
    // always the caller's own ground truth (PassageList's own _blocks.Count,
    // CatechismSeamSection's own items.Count).
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public int Total { get; set; }

    // The floor collapsing never crosses -- the SAME number the list's own
    // initial render already shows (PassageList's existing Cap parameter,
    // CatechismSeamSection's own new 2, F2/CAP-RECONCILE-1's 2-vs-3 xrefs
    // rule) -- "never below the default," the owner's own words.
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public int Default { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public int Step { get; set; } = 2;

    // Two-way bound -- the caller owns the actual state (its own _shown
    // field), this component only ever proposes the next value via
    // ShownChanged, never mutates anything itself (a stateless control,
    // matching PassageList.razor's/MiniReaderExpand.razor's own established
    // "per-render state lives in the CALLER, not silently duplicated in a
    // shared child" discipline).
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public int Shown { get; set; }
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public EventCallback<int> ShownChanged { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public string MoreTestId { get; set; } = "reveal-more";
    [Parameter] public string CollapseTestId { get; set; } = "reveal-collapse";
    [Parameter] public string RevealNoun { get; set; } = "entries";

    private Task OnMoreClick(MouseEventArgs e) =>
        ShownChanged.InvokeAsync(e.ShiftKey ? Total : Math.Min(Shown + Step, Total));

    private Task OnCollapseClick(MouseEventArgs e) =>
        ShownChanged.InvokeAsync(e.ShiftKey ? Default : Math.Max(Shown - Step, Default));
}
